  «text (2950,-1200) 1.6 7 0 0x1000000 -1 -1 "ï»¿.tran 30m"»
  «text (-3750,6250) 3 7 1 0x1000000 -1 -1 "ï»¿Traffic Signal State Machine using Verilog FSM in QSPICE"»
  «text (-3733,3943) 2 7 1 0x1000000 -1 -1 "ï»¿4-state state machine controlling a 4-way intersection with pedestrian \nwalk button. FSM written in Verilog, running as a hierarchical block in \nQSPICE, driving 6 LEDs.\n\nTransitions: S0 (Main Green) â†’ S1 (Main Amber, 4ms) â†’ S2 (Cross Green, 8ms) \n           â†’ S3 (Cross Amber, 4ms) â†’ S0\n\nClock scaled 1 kHz for simulation (1 ms = 1 sec real-world timing)."»
  «text (4700,-950) 1.6 7 0 0x1000000 -1 -1 "ï»¿.plot V(main_green) V(main_amber)+1.1 V(main_red)+2.2"»
  «text (4650,-1200) 1.6 7 0 0x1000000 -1 -1 "ï»¿.plot V(cross_green) V(cross_amber)+1.1 V(cross_red)+2.2"»
  «text (-4800,-2650) 2 7 1 0x1000000 -1 -1 "ï»¿Building the .dll from source:\n1. Grab "traffic_fsm.qsch" and "traffic_fsm_x1.v" from my repo:\n   https://github.com/kelvinthiha17/traffic-signal-fsm-qspice\n2. Right-click on X1, then go to Verilog Interface --> Open Verilog Source\n3. In the source window, right-click and select Compile Verilog to C++\n4. Run the simulation as usual"»
»

